::ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is log<sub>2</sub>(16)? {
= 4#Correct. The logarithm asks what power of 2 equals 16, and 2<sup>4</sup> = 16.
~ 2#2<sup>2</sup> = 4, not 16.
~ 8#2<sup>8</sup> = 256, not 16.
~ 32#This is not an exponent that makes 2 equal 16.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation is equivalent to log<sub>3</sub>(27)=3? {
~ 27<sup>3</sup> = 3#This reverses the base and input.
= 3<sup>3</sup> = 27#Correct. A logarithm statement rewrites as base raised to output equals input.
~ 3 + 27 = 30#Logarithms are about exponents, not addition.
~ 27/3 = 9#Division does not represent the logarithmic relationship.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the domain of y = log<sub>5</sub>(x)? {
~ all real numbers#Logarithmic inputs must be positive.
~ x &ge; 0#x = 0 is not included because no positive base power equals 0.
= x &gt; 0#Correct. A basic logarithmic function only accepts positive inputs.
~ x &ne; 5#The base does not create a domain restriction like a denominator.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement describes logarithms? {
~ A logarithm gives a slope.#Slope is a rate of change, not the meaning of a logarithm.
~ A logarithm gives a y-intercept.#A logarithm can have intercepts, but that is not its definition.
~ A logarithm gives a denominator restriction.#That confuses logs with rational expressions.
= A logarithm gives an exponent.#Correct. log<sub>b</sub>(a) asks what exponent on b produces a.
}

::ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L04_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.2<br><br>Question:<br>The graph of y = log<sub>2</sub>(x) is the inverse of which graph? {
= y = 2<sup>x</sup>#Correct. Logarithmic and exponential functions with the same base are inverses.
~ y = x<sup>2</sup>#A quadratic is not the matching exponential inverse.
~ y = x/2#This is linear.
~ y = 2x#This is linear, not exponential.
}

